<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albrecht Dürer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1471">1471</a>–<a href="/years/1528">1528</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albrecht Dürer was a painter, printmaker and theorist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, the leading artist of the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His woodcuts and engravings, such as the Apocalypse series, Melencolia I and the Rhinoceros, spread his fame across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and his journeys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> brought Renaissance ideas north. He also wrote treatises on geometry and human proportion and became a supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer.jpg" alt="Albrecht Dürer" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albrecht Dürer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%BCrer_Alte_Pinakothek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dürer was born on 21 May 1471, the third child and second son of Albrecht Dürer the Elder, a successful goldsmith who had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> from Ajtós in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> by 1455, and Barbara Holper. Of the couple's eighteen children only three survived. After his apprenticeship Dürer took the customary Wanderjahre, about four years away from 1490. He may have hoped to work under the engraver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-schongauer">Martin Schongauer</a>, who died shortly before Dürer reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> in 1492, and he stayed with Schongauer's brothers there and in Basel. In 1493 he went to Strasbourg, where he painted his first self-portrait, probably to send to his fiancée in Nuremberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Within three months of his marriage Dürer left alone for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, perhaps because of plague in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, and studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, where he admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-bellini">Giovanni Bellini</a> and copied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-mantegna">Andrea Mantegna</a>. On his return in 1495 he opened his own workshop. His early woodcuts were larger, more finely cut and more complex in composition than most German woodcuts before them. It is now thought unlikely that he cut the blocks himself, a task for specialist craftsmen. On a second Italian journey, from 1505 to 1507, he returned to painting and made the altarpiece known as the Feast of the Rosary for the German community in Venice.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> from mid-1507 he painted Adam and Eve (1507) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assumption-of-mary">Assumption of the Virgin</a> (1509) and completed the Great Passion and the Life of the Virgin woodcut series, both published in 1511. In July 1520, after the death of Emperor Maximilian I, he travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to renew the imperial pension and to win the favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. His diary records to whom he gave, exchanged or sold prints, and shows that the trip was not profitable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In his last years Dürer produced comparatively little as an artist, partly through declining health and partly because he was preparing treatises. His Four Books on Measurement dealt with geometry, from lines and regular polygons to architecture, typography and polyhedra. The Four Books on Human Proportion, of 1528, expressed the body's parts as fractions of its height, based in part on observation of two to three hundred living persons in his own words. In 1527 he published a book on the fortification of cities. He wrote in German, using craftsmen's language, rather than in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. His prints were the medium through which most contemporaries knew his art, and they influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmigianino">Parmigianino</a> to promote their own work through printmakers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albrecht Dürer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Albrecht Dürer</b>, Veit Stoss, Hans Sachs and Martin Behaim, maker of the oldest surviving globe, worked there.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Free Imperial City of Nuremberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.45,11.08&amp;year=1508" aria-label="Nuremberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Albrecht Dürer"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5612 5845h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5612 5845h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.45,11.08&amp;year=1508">What was here in 1508? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O God, if Luther be dead, who will henceforth expound the Holy Gospel so clearly to us! Ah, God, what might he not have written for us in the next ten or twenty years!&#8221;</p><footer>Diary of the journey to the Netherlands, May 1521 &#183; tr. Rudolf Tombo, 1913 &#183; on the false news of Luther's death after Worms &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3226/pg3226-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…all the days of my life I have seen nothing that reaches my heart so much as these, for among them I have seen wonderfully artistic things and have admired the subtle ingenuity of men in foreign lands…&#8221;</p><footer>Diary of the journey to the Netherlands, 1520, on the Aztec treasures sent to Charles V &#183; tr. Rudolf Tombo, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3226/pg3226-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Hear, thou knight of Christ, ride on beside the Lord Jesus; guard the truth, win the martyr's crown!&#8221;</p><footer>Diary of the journey to the Netherlands, May 1521, an appeal to Erasmus &#183; tr. Rudolf Tombo, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3226/pg3226-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
